Selected filesystem backport regression models
==============================================

test_backport.py provides single-threaded, extracted-source host tests, NOT
kernel runtime selftests. The additional test_f2fs_merge.py uses pthread mock
workers as described below. Run from the source tree with a host Clang compiler:

    python3 tools/testing/selftests/fs_backport/test_backport.py --cc clang
    python3 tools/testing/selftests/fs_backport/test_backport.py \
        --cc 'clang -fsanitize=undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all'

HOSTCC also sets the compiler. A multiword compiler/wrapper command is accepted.
No source or configuration is modified; generated C and binaries use a temporary
directory. No external Python modules are required. Run manually; this directory
is intentionally not connected to the kernel's in-kernel test infrastructure.

Scope and provenance
--------------------

* F2FS compression reserve/release dnode operations hold the checkpoint operation
  read lock across lookup, mutation and dnode release, with balanced error exits.
  Donor: 45c506bd3b8de8e79e09b4350354b211ab498aeb.
  Upstream: 0a4ed2d97cb6d044196cc3e726b6699222b41019.
* F2FS GC rejects an inline-data inode found with an external data block, marks
  NEED_FSCK and logs BEFORE releasing its inode reference. This is a corrected
  local adaptation, not a verbatim donor import, and does not repair corruption.
  Donor: d287de5bde53a9777d9bf68a166102fa03f7b92a.
  Upstream: fc01008c92f40015aeeced94750855a7111b6929.
* F2FS move-range and pin-file ioctls reject atomic files after inode locking and
  use existing cleanup labels. The existing defragmentation exclusion remains.
  Donor: 00ad9f9442869ff7cb38f5329f9e1f6a3ce684d6.
  Upstream: bfe5c02654261bfb8bd9cb174a67f3279ea99e58.
* Epoll takes only a positive-count temporary file reference before polling and
  balances fput. The local missing vfs_poll helper has DEFAULT_POLLMASK fallback.
  This does NOT resurrect a file whose reference count has reached zero.
  Donor: 655ae9dbb75a0c11932f8a8a728fdb7c8b9f323d.
  Upstream: 4efaa5acf0a1d2b5947f98abb3acf8bfd966422b.
* Writeback leaves freeing-inode lists untouched. The completion path removes
  only clean, non-I_FREEING inodes. The late __mark_inode_dirty check precedes
  list movement after the inode-lock/list-lock handoff; it never deletes a
  freeing inode's list entry. Existing early guards/dirtytime behavior remain.
  This deliberately does NOT copy the donor's forced-deletion predicate.
  Donor: 7209a1acc23b95bbee4bfee238e2fac417d2709a.
  Upstream intent: 4e3c51f4e805291b057d12f5dda5aeb50a538dc4.
  The newer upstream dirtytime requeue path is absent here; this adaptation is
  defensive hardening, not a claim to reproduce that exact upstream regression.

Not included: F2FS checkpoint tuning (10c2478e), optional epoll RCU-delayed
storage freeing (e041ee9c), the bulk F2FS refresh, or device configuration changes.

Coverage and limits
-------------------

test_backport.py extracts current production functions/branches into model.c:

* Complete writeback_single_inode and __mark_inode_dirty functions plus both
  cgroup and non-cgroup inode/list-lock helpers: 16,384 completion cases and
  46,080 dirty-state/handoff cases PER helper variant. Checks freeing-list
  exclusion, lock/reference balance, redirty/clean handling and legacy dirtytime.
* Complete epi_fget, ep_item_poll and vfs_poll: 4,096 combinations per variant,
  including zero/live references, masks, missing poll operation and a simulated
  close during polling. Checks the temporary pin and balanced release.
* Both compression dnode loops: 2,592 cases per variant across success, ENOENT,
  lookup failure and mutation failure; checks lock/dnode balance and accounting.
* The GC inline-data guard: owned-reference logging/release and skip behavior.
* The move-range lock/atomic guard and cleanup, and pin-file atomic guard and
  cleanup: 18 cases per variant, including trylock failure and same inode.

For GC/ioctls/compression these are extracted BRANCH/LOOP models, not complete
ioctl or filesystem executions. Mock operations do not access a filesystem.
The cgroup tests exercise both real handoff helper implementations with a
simulated state change during unlock, not concurrent wb association migration.

--negative accepts no-file-pin, missing-fput, donor-freeing, late-freeing,
no-cp-lock, gc-after-iput or atomic-bypass. Each deliberately mutates extracted
code in memory; expect a runtime assertion failure (nonzero exit), not a passing
test. They validate sensitivity to the named missing/broken protection.

Local validation also compiled the five affected/dependent ARM64 objects with
ThinLTO in the device-derived configuration, and six objects (including
fs/f2fs/compress.o) in a separate compression+cgroup-writeback+debug/lockdep
configuration. Both relocatable partial links passed using Clang 19.1.7/LLD.
The device defconfig is unchanged; compression remains disabled there.

These results do not establish SMP ordering, runtime lockdep, actual concurrent
file teardown, mount/recovery correctness, compression IO correctness, or a
full vmlinux/image build or boot. They do not measure phone performance/battery
life. The production AOSP compiler was not used for these local checks.

Selective F2FS merge tests
--------------------------

    python3 tools/testing/selftests/fs_backport/test_f2fs_merge.py --cc clang

--stage A, B1 or B2 selects cumulative checks (default B2). The IPU functions,
GC admission/freezer helpers, and CP worker/request/start/stop functions are
extracted from current production sources. Source guards also cover default-off
options, remount cleanup, error paths and retained prior protections. The GC
worker loop itself is source-checked, not executed as a kernel worker.

The harness uses mock kernel primitives, pthreads, assertions and nonrecovering
undefined-behavior traps: 800 GC requests, 1,200 CP requests and targeted IPU,
late-freezer and quota/freeze fallback cases per full run. Assertions are
explicitly enabled even with compilers that define NDEBUG for optimized builds.
No mounted filesystem, block device or user data is accessed. Compiler products
are temporary. These are not kernel concurrency, lockdep or durability tests.

--negative accepts null-selector, missing-accounting, bounce-accounting,
no-ipu-drain, gc-freezer-window, cp-late-counter, cp-no-umount-protection,
cp-no-lifetime-gate or cp-drop-error. Unlike the older script's --negative,
this script compiles the chosen broken variant and EXPECTS an assertion/trap;
it succeeds only when that mutation is rejected at runtime. For example:

    python3 tools/testing/selftests/fs_backport/test_f2fs_merge.py \
        --cc clang --negative cp-no-lifetime-gate

See Documentation/filesystems/f2fs-staged/implementation.md and UPSTREAM.json
for exact boundaries, local ARM64 build configurations, provenance and limits.
The donor's full filesystem refresh and default mount-policy changes remain
excluded; the GC/checkpoint merge options are implemented but default off.
